EXAMPLES=[{'id':'we-6-source-9','num':'9','title':'Example 9','problem':r'Evaluate \(\int (x+2)/\sqrt{x^2+4x+5}\,dx\).','given':'A positive completed-square denominator.','method':'Substitute u = x+2, then use a simple inner derivative; compare the source trigonometric substitution.','steps':['Complete the square: x²+4x+5 = (x+2)²+1, positive for every real x.','Put u = x+2, du = dx. The integral becomes ∫u/√(u²+1) du.','Differentiate √(u²+1): the chain rule gives u/√(u²+1).','Therefore an antiderivative is √(u²+1)+C.','Return to x: √((x+2)²+1)+C = √(x²+4x+5)+C.','The source route u = tan θ gives ∫tan θsec θ dθ = sec θ+C, with θ in (−π/2,π/2).','On that interval sec θ is positive and equals √(1+u²), giving the same result. Differentiating the answer checks the original integrand on all ℝ.'],'answer':'√(x²+4x+5)+C.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':158,'pageEnd':159,'verification':'complete source question and solution checked'}]}, {'id':'we-6-source-10','num':'10','title':'Example 10','problem':r'Evaluate \(\int xe^x\,dx\).','given':'A product of an algebraic and an exponential factor.','method':'Integration by parts, choosing u = x and dv = eˣdx.','steps':['The integration-by-parts rule is ∫u dv = uv−∫v du.','Choose u = x so that du = dx.','Choose dv = eˣdx, giving v = eˣ.','Substitute to obtain ∫xeˣdx = xeˣ−∫eˣdx.','The remaining integral is eˣ+C.','Thus the result is xeˣ−eˣ+C = eˣ(x−1)+C.','Check: [eˣ(x−1)]′ = eˣ(x−1)+eˣ = xeˣ. The formula is valid for every real x.'],'answer':'xeˣ−eˣ+C.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':159,'verification':'full source question and working checked'}]}]
